// Main Differences
// Feature	MongoDB Native Driver	Mongoose
// Level	Low level	High level
// Schema	No schema	Schema based
// Validation	Manually write	Built-in validation
// Code size	More code	Less code
// Performance	Slightly faster	Slight overhead
// Learning	Harder	Easier
// Relationships	Manual handling	Easier with populate
// Middleware	No	Yes
// Best for	Simple apps, scripts	Express/MERN applications
// Example Difference

// Suppose you want age should always be a number.

// Native Driver

// You can insert:

// {
//  name:"Somya",
//  age:"hello"
// }

// MongoDB accepts it.

// You have to manually check:

// if(typeof age !== "number"){
// throw Error();
// }
// Mongoose

// Schema:

// const studentSchema=new mongoose.Schema({

// name:{
// type:String,
// required:true
// },

// age:{
// type:Number,
// required:true
// }

// });

// Now Mongoose automatically validates:

// age:"hello"

// will give an error.

// Which one is better?

// For MERN stack / React + Node + Express projects:

// ✅ Mongoose is preferred

// Because:

// Easier CRUD operations
// Schema validation
// Cleaner code
// Industry standard
// Easier interviews

// Most companies using MongoDB with Express use:

// React
//  |
// Node.js
//  |
// Express.js
//  |
// Mongoose
//  |
// MongoDB

// For your current CRUD student application, I would recommend using Mongoose because you are preparing for frontend/full-stack interviews. It will help you learn:

// Models
// Schema
// Validation
// Middleware
// Relationships (populate)
// Better project structure

// Your current MongoClient knowledge is still useful because it teaches how MongoDB works internally. But for MERN projects, learn Mongoose next.